ostomus.hom_jo","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":24,"p":[7,7],"r":[8,39],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings viii. 39"},{"id":"a05e09edaca4","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_jo","w":"chrysostomus.hom_jo","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":31,"p":[11,11],"r":[16,24],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings xvi. 24"},{"id":"a1581625c2ee","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_jo","w":"chrysostomus.hom_jo","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":38,"p":[3,3],"r":[15,23],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings xv. 23"},{"id":"3822cb29c12e","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_jo","w":"chrysostomus.hom_jo","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":88,"p":[21,21],"r":[21,27],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings xxi. 27"},{"id":"5eeee33ec47d","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_act","w":"chrysostomus.hom_act","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":4,"p":[4,4],"r":[19,21],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings xix. 21"},{"id":"e44633eab6b8","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_act","w":"chrysostomus.hom_act","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":12,"p":[6,6],"r":[21,19],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings xxi. 19"},{"id":"b438b93e05e4","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_act","w":"chrysostomus.hom_act","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":17,"p":[2,2],"r":[12,28],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"It is not probable that this passage (v. 39, 40) means that the people proposed to return to Egypt (as Chrys.). In the O. T. the constant representation is that the golden calf (or bull) was worshipped as the image of the divinity who had led them out of Egypt (Ex. xxxii. 4; 1 Kings xii. 28). It seems clearly implied in Ezek. xx. 7, Ezek. xx. 8, Ezek. xx. 24, that the Israelites while in Egypt had been much addicted to the idolatry of the country. The meaning here is that, being discouraged and disappointed on account of Moses' continued absence in the mount, they were ready to transfer their allegiance from Jehovah to some of the divinities to whose worship they had previously been accustomed. The worship of cattle was especially common, as of Apis at Memphis and Mnevis at Heliopolis.-G. B. S."},{"id":"074b1d8a327f","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_act","w":"chrysostomus.hom_act","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":17,"p":[3,3],"r":[19,10],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings xix. 10"},{"id":"c6c8642651b0","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_act","w":"chrysostomus.hom_act","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":17,"p":[4,4],"r":[18,21],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings xviii. 21"},{"id":"c0ab34091435","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_act","w":"chrysostomus.hom_act","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":29,"p":[1,1],"r":[6,1],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Upon the reading of the T. R. (A. V.) the period of the Judges is here stated to have been 450 years. This agrees with the chronology of the book of Judges and of Josephus, but conflicts with 1 Kings vi. 1 where we are told that \"in the four hundred and eightieth year after the children of Israel were come out of the land of Egypt, in the fourth year of Solomon's reign over Israel, he began to build the house of the Lord.\" This would give but 331 years for the period of the Judges. It is the view of many critics that Paul has here followed a different chronology from that of 1 Kings which was also in use among the Jews and was followed by Josephus (so Meyer.) But if the reading of Tischendorf, Lechler, and Westcott and Hort (R. V.) is adopted-and it is sustained by A. b.c. )"},{"id":"bd0fe833d8d7","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_act","w":"chrysostomus.hom_act","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":31,"p":[3,3],"r":[19,10],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings xix. 10"},{"id":"34dbf3d83607","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_act","w":"chrysostomus.hom_act","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":53,"p":[2,2],"r":[17,17],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings xvii. 17"},{"id":"a88e446e4448","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_rom","w":"chrysostomus.hom_rom","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":2,"p":[25,25],"r":[20,35],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings xx. 35"},{"id":"37c8379c82bd","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_rom","w":"chrysostomus.hom_rom","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":17,"p":[18,18],"r":[18,21],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings xviii. 21"},{"id":"417bbfeb2b04","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_rom","w":"chrysostomus.hom_rom","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":18,"p":[22,22],"r":[19,14],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"Referring to his words, 1 Kings xix. 14, and to his sharing in the famine, xvii. 13."},{"id":"0df2383eae49","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_rom","w":"chrysostomus.hom_rom","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":18,"p":[22,22],"r":[19,18],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings xix. 18"},{"id":"dd4ff4c0dce3","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_rom","w":"chrysostomus.hom_rom","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":25,"p":[19,19],"r":[21,23],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings xxi. 23"},{"id":"e336d65477d6","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_rom","w":"chrysostomus.hom_rom","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":25,"p":[19,19],"r":[21,25],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings xxi. 25"},{"id":"b70955be9aac","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_rom","w":"chrysostomus.hom_rom","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":25,"p":[19,19],"r":[21,29],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings xxi. 29"},{"id":"2f9ffa91b4bc","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_1co","w":"chrysostomus.hom_1co","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":5,"p":[23,23],"r":[19,10],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings chapter 19, verse 10"},{"id":"6b8f266341de","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_2co","w":"chrysostomus.hom_2co","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":4,"p":[48,48],"r":[21,29],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings chapter 21, verse 29"},{"id":"9752cdd71c6a","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_2co","w":"chrysostomus.hom_2co","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":19,"p":[23,23],"r":[17,12],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings chapter 17, verse 12"},{"id":"6f7eacd9a825","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_2co","w":"chrysostomus.hom_2co","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":26,"p":[11,11],"r":[5,18],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"medium","cited":"1 Kings chapter 5, verse 4","note":"Stellenangabe der Ausgabe (englische Zählung, umgerechnet)"},{"id":"ef9ab64af4b0","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_2co","w":"chrysostomus.hom_2co","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":28,"p":[14,14],"r":[21,20],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings chapter 21, verse 20"},{"id":"d9c432fb79df","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_phil","w":"chrysostomus.hom_phil","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":1,"p":[20,20],"r":[17,14],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings xvii. 14"},{"id":"dbc18536cbb7","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_phil","w":"chrysostomus.hom_phil","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":1,"p":[20,20],"r":[17,12],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings xvii. 12"},{"id":"bf22c7aa14a1","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_phil","w":"chrysostomus.hom_phil","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":5,"p":[15,15],"r":[18,18],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings xviii. 18"},{"id":"b6b6d9f65064","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_phil","w":"chrysostomus.hom_phil","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":5,"p":[15,15],"r":[18,21],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings xviii. 21"},{"id":"22f80b648624","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_1th","w":"chrysostomus.hom_1th","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":4,"p":[2,2],"r":[19,10],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings xix. 10"},{"id":"7a344940b22c","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_2tim","w":"chrysostomus.hom_2tim","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":8,"p":[36,36],"r":[18,21],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings xviii. 21"},{"id":"3eb8d0a4f0b6","v":"npnf.chrysostomus.hom_heb","w":"chrysostomus.hom_heb","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":18,"p":[16,16],"r":[18,18],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings xviii. 18"},{"id":"ac21f92d7040","v":"allen.chrysostomus.de_lazaro","w":"chrysostomus.de_lazaro","a":"johannes_chrysostomus","s":3,"p":[30,30],"r":[19,4],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings xix. 4"},{"id":"15ea58844c5c","v":"npnf.basilius.epistulae","w":"basilius.epistulae","a":"basilius_von_casarea","s":210,"p":[6,6],"r":[22,22],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings xxii. 22."},{"id":"997cf27b233f","v":"npnf.ambrosius.de_spiritu_sancto","w":"ambrosius.de_spiritu_sancto","a":"ambrosius_von_mailand","s":25,"p":[2,2],"r":[18,45],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"It has been thought well in translating this verse to keep the words \"spirit\" and \"breath\" as suiting the argument of St. Ambrose. But there can be little doubt that the ordinary translation is the correct one. Bp. Westcott has the following note: \"In Hebrew, Syriac, and Latin the words [for spirit and wind] are identical, and Wiclif and the Rhemish version keep \"spirit\" in both cases, after the Latin. But at present the retention of one word in both places could only create confusion, since the separation between the material emblem and the power which it was used to describe is complete. The use of the correlative verb ( pnei pneuma ), and still at the same time the whole of the phraseology is inspired by the higher meaning. Perhaps also the unusual word ( pneuma , 1 Kings xviii. 45; 1 Kings xix. 11; 2 Kings iii. 17) is employed to suggest this. The comparison lies between the obvious physical properties of the wind and the mysterious action of that spiritual influence to which the name \"spirit,\" \"wind,\" was instinctively applied. The laws of both are practically unknown, both are unseen, the presence of both is revealed in their effects.\"-Westcott on S. John iii. 8."},{"id":"30bd1fd0cf88","v":"npnf.ambrosius.de_spiritu_sancto","w":"ambrosius.de_spiritu_sancto","a":"ambrosius_von_mailand","s":25,"p":[2,2],"r":[19,11],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"It has been thought well in translating this verse to keep the words \"spirit\" and \"breath\" as suiting the argument of St. Ambrose. But there can be little doubt that the ordinary translation is the correct one. Bp. Westcott has the following note: \"In Hebrew, Syriac, and Latin the words [for spirit and wind] are identical, and Wiclif and the Rhemish version keep \"spirit\" in both cases, after the Latin. But at present the retention of one word in both places could only create confusion, since the separation between the material emblem and the power which it was used to describe is complete. The use of the correlative verb ( pnei pneuma ), and still at the same time the whole of the phraseology is inspired by the higher meaning. Perhaps also the unusual word ( pneuma , 1 Kings xviii. 45; 1 Kings xix. 11; 2 Kings iii. 17) is employed to suggest this. The comparison lies between the obvious physical properties of the wind and the mysterious action of that spiritual influence to which the name \"spirit,\" \"wind,\" was instinctively applied. The laws of both are practically unknown, both are unseen, the presence of both is revealed in their effects.\"-Westcott on S. John iii. 8."},{"id":"04b4e9120d8e","v":"npnf.ambrosius.de_excessu_fratris","w":"ambrosius.de_excessu_fratris","a":"ambrosius_von_mailand","s":2,"p":[126,126],"r":[19,4],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"high","cited":"1 Kings xix. 4."},{"id":"e87ea4515af7","v":"npnf.ambrosius.de_fide","w":"ambrosius.de_fide","a":"ambrosius_von_mailand","s":1,"p":[1,1],"r":[10,1],"k":"quotation","m":"editionCitation","c":"